.. raw:: html

    <div class="sphx-glr-thumbcontainer" tooltip="Jean-Pierre Berenger solved a problem that had limited FDTD simulations since Yee&#x27;s original 1966 paper: a finite grid needs some boundary condition, and a simple wall reflects outgoing waves right back into the simulation. His Perfectly Matched Layer (PML) is a graded absorbing medium whose wave impedance matches the interior exactly, so outgoing waves are absorbed rather than reflected. pml_conductivity_profile implements a simplified (non-split-field) approximation to this idea: it grades an ordinary electric conductivity \sigma(x) smoothly up from zero over the outermost cells at each edge of the grid, so E_z there obeys the lossy telegraph-style update of a conducting medium rather than the lossless Ampere&#x27;s law,">

.. only:: html

  .. image:: /api/gallery/fields/electrodynamics/images/thumb/sphx_glr_plot_pml_absorbing_boundary_thumb.png
    :alt:

  :doc:`/api/gallery/fields/electrodynamics/plot_pml_absorbing_boundary`

.. raw:: html

      <div class="sphx-glr-thumbnail-title">Berenger's Perfectly Matched Layer vs. a hard wall</div>
    </div>


.. only:: not html

 * :doc:`/api/gallery/fields/electrodynamics/plot_pml_absorbing_boundary`

.. raw:: html

    <div class="sphx-glr-thumbcontainer" tooltip="Kane Yee&#x27;s 1966 idea was deceptively simple: stagger the electric and magnetic field components on interleaved spatial and temporal grids (a &quot;Yee cell&quot;), so each field&#x27;s curl is naturally centered on the other&#x27;s location. fdtd_1d and fdtd_2d_tmz are direct implementations of this staggered-grid leapfrog scheme for the TMz-mode Maxwell curl equations,">

.. only:: html

  .. image:: /api/gallery/fields/electrodynamics/images/thumb/sphx_glr_plot_yee_fdtd_point_source_thumb.png
